WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.367
- Welcome to Larry King Now,

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.937
our special guest 
is Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.939
the self-proclaimed hustler,

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:09.743
is a digital media mogul,
author, web show host,

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:12.913
and venture capitalist
among many other things.

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:16.683
As the CEO and 
co-founder of VaynerMedia,

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:19.853
Gary hosts the hugely
popular YouTube show,

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.288
#AskGaryVee.

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:24.491
And has penned three New York
Times best selling books.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:28.595
Gary has been named to Fortune
Magazine's 40 Under 40 list,

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:30.898
of the most influential 
business leaders,

00:00:30.898 --> 00:00:33.033
and holds the 
number one ranking

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.360
on Forbes top 40 social
selling market masters.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:42.715
His newest book, 
#AskGaryVee, is available now.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.213
How did this all 
start, you, wine?

00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:47.080
(laughs)

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.782
What, what happened with you?

00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:50.250
- What happened with me is,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.920
I had the great benefit
of being an immigrant.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.355
I was born in Belarus, 
in the former Soviet Union.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:56.857
- [Larry] My mother 
was from Belarus.

00:00:56.857 --> 00:00:57.658
- I didn't know that.

00:00:57.658 --> 00:00:58.592
- Minsk I think.

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.661
- Yeah, I was born 
40 minutes from Minsk.

00:01:00.661 --> 00:01:03.196
And came to the states in '78,

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.232
when they let some 
Jews out of there.

00:01:05.232 --> 00:01:08.411
And, we set up in Queens.

00:01:08.411 --> 00:01:10.070
And my parents lived 
the American dream,

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.371
they worked very hard.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.673
My dad was a 
stock boy in a liquor store

00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:14.374
in Clark, New Jersey.

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.343
And eventually became the
manager of that store,

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:17.778
and eventually saved 
up enough money

00:01:17.778 --> 00:01:20.280
to buy a store in 
Springfield, New Jersey.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.383
I was lemonade stands, baseball
cards, real hustler kid,

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:26.286
Blow Pops, 
anything to make a buck.

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.655
And at 14, I got dragged 
into the store.

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:30.190
You know, oldest son, 
immigrant family.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.458
I always tell people, Larry,

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:33.493
that I lived their 
grandparent's life

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.661
more than theirs, right?

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:36.797
I'm couple generations 
behind most.

00:01:36.797 --> 00:01:38.732
I did it in the 70's, 
and 80's, and 90's,

00:01:38.732 --> 00:01:41.702
when most people did in
the 30's, 40's, and 50's.

00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:42.602
- You're a legal immigrant?

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:43.704
- I am, thank God.

00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:44.538
- [Larry] Okay.

00:01:44.538 --> 00:01:45.839
- Otherwise I probably
wouldn't do the show--

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:46.673
- Donald?

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:47.607
Okay.
(laughs)

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.744
- And, I fell in love with
people collecting wine

00:01:50.744 --> 00:01:51.878
when I was 17,

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:54.114
because I was into
collecting sports cards.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.949
That was my connection point.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:56.783
I wanted--

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:57.777
- Collecting?
- Collecting.

00:01:57.777 --> 00:02:00.887
I wanted to build 
4,000 wine shops.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:03.223
That was, I was gonna build
a Toys "R" Us of wine,

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:05.092
sell the franchise, 
buy the New York Jets.

00:02:05.092 --> 00:02:07.194
That's what the plan was.

00:02:07.194 --> 00:02:09.596
Heard the internet my
freshman year of college,

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:10.630
heard that sound,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.499
cuh, cuh, chee, cuh.

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.101
Knew that it was special.

00:02:14.101 --> 00:02:16.203
And in 1996, I launched 
one of the first

00:02:16.203 --> 00:02:18.338
e-commerce wine 
businesses in America.

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:20.207
Called WineLibrary.com.

00:02:20.207 --> 00:02:21.608
Took over my dad's business,

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:23.677
kind of running it 
day to day in 1998,

00:02:23.677 --> 00:02:24.711
alongside with him.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:28.248
And from '98 to 2003,
helped grow that business

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.784
from a three to a 
$60 million business.

00:02:30.784 --> 00:02:32.919
That became the foundation.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.589
Built that on e-commerce, 
email marketing,

00:02:35.589 --> 00:02:37.157
banner advertising, 
Google AdWords,

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:40.394
things that the marketing
world didn't believe yet.

00:02:40.394 --> 00:02:42.763
And then, YouTube came out.

00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:46.333
And I started a wine show four
months after YouTube started.

00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:47.167
And that--

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:48.235
- You are not a wine expert?

00:02:48.235 --> 00:02:49.803
- I grew up a wine expert.

00:02:49.803 --> 00:02:52.506
You know, from 15 to 30,

00:02:52.506 --> 00:02:54.741
in those 15 years, 
my whole life was wine.

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.110
- Were you always successful?

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.079
- In everything but school.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:02:59.946
- Didn't do well in school?

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:00.781
- Poor.

00:03:00.781 --> 00:03:03.022
Terrible actually.

00:03:03.022 --> 00:03:03.950
Punted it.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.452
You know, it was funny.

00:03:05.452 --> 00:03:07.788
And this is where I give
my parents enormous credit,

00:03:07.788 --> 00:03:08.822
and I've, you know it's funny,

00:03:08.822 --> 00:03:11.158
it's a business book
that says self-awareness.

00:03:11.158 --> 00:03:13.693
My parents grew up, and 
I give them so much credit,

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.063
in a world where all 
their contemporaries,

00:03:16.063 --> 00:03:17.697
as, and you know this,

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.666
education's the way 
out for immigrants.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:20.500
- Sure is.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.270
- My mom recognized 
that I was a merchant,

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.006
an entrepreneur, a promoter.

00:03:26.006 --> 00:03:28.108
- So did school fail you,
or you failed school?

00:03:28.108 --> 00:03:29.242
- School failed me.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:31.845
School's failing entrepreneurs
every single day.

00:03:31.845 --> 00:03:32.679
- Because?

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.682
- Because it's not built 
for entrepreneurship.

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.384
It's built for workers.

00:03:37.384 --> 00:03:40.887
You know, if, you're being
taught to play within the lines.

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.556
And there's nothing 
being taught that maps

00:03:42.556 --> 00:03:44.591
to the entrepreneurial market.

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.260
As a matter of fact, my biggest
cynicism when I sit across

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.562
an entrepreneur today,

00:03:48.562 --> 00:03:51.431
is if they are too 
successful at school.

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:53.266
I probably look at 
Ivy League grads

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.768
starting startups right now

00:03:54.768 --> 00:03:56.603
with more of a negative light,

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:58.438
than I do somebody 
who wasn't as good.

00:03:58.438 --> 00:03:59.272
- Because?

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:00.307
- Because what I've learned

00:04:00.307 --> 00:04:01.708
over the last five 
to seven years,

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.009
and by the way, in the
last two, three years,

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.331
I've taken a step back on this,

00:04:04.331 --> 00:04:06.613
because there's too many
entrepreneurial friends

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:08.548
who've gone to great schools
that have been successful,

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:09.850
so this is not a 
blanket statement.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.053
But I will tell you that in
a world of private schools,

00:04:13.053 --> 00:04:16.089
in a world of mommy and daddy
having a lot of connections,

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.625
that when you go from 12, 15,
18 years of that ecosystem,

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:21.995
and you go into a market,
and you create an app,

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:26.399
the market doesn't give
a crap who your dad is.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.001
The market responds 
to your product,

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:29.803
and a lot of these kids 
have not been able

00:04:29.803 --> 00:04:31.204
to take the punch in the mouth

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:33.240
that comes along with
entrepreneurship.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.775
- You're big on 
self-awareness, right?

00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:35.609
- [Gary] Huge.

00:04:35.609 --> 00:04:37.677
- How does one get 
to be self-aware?

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:39.146
- I don't know.

00:04:39.146 --> 00:04:40.380
- So how do you teach it?

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.681
- I don't know.

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.283
But I know it's damn important.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:46.386
And so, I know where I start
and where I stop, Larry.

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:47.220
And I wish,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.855
honestly I'm curious to see

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:50.323
over the next 40, 
50 years of my career,

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.626
if I figure it out.

00:04:52.626 --> 00:04:54.294
I think that,

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:56.229
the things that I've been
pushing people to do is,

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:58.765
one, create an ecosystem
where you make the people

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:02.035
closest to you feel comfortable
to tell you the truth.

00:05:02.035 --> 00:05:03.804
So, one of the things I've
been asking for people to do

00:05:03.804 --> 00:05:06.807
is tell your mom 
and dad and spouse,

00:05:06.807 --> 00:05:08.408
best friend, coworker,

00:05:08.408 --> 00:05:10.310
hey, tell me the truth.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.678
What am I good at, 
what am I bad at?

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:13.780
And spend a month or two
to get them comfortable

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.248
to actually tell you the truth.

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:16.082
Cause the people that love you

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:17.450
sure don't want to tell you.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.986
- How did the website 
thing come about?

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.054
- Way back when?

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:22.022
- I mean, you, your web show.

00:05:22.022 --> 00:05:23.166
- [Gary] Or the web show now?

00:05:23.166 --> 00:05:25.102
- What is the biggest thing
you do, is your web show?

00:05:25.102 --> 00:05:26.493
- The biggest thing 
I do right now

00:05:26.493 --> 00:05:31.164
is I run a 650 person
social media digital agency

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.366
that works with the brands
like Toyota, and Pepsi--

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:34.201
- [Larry] That's called?

00:05:34.201 --> 00:05:35.235
- And that's called VaynerMedia.

00:05:35.235 --> 00:05:36.503
- And what does VaynerMedia do?

00:05:36.503 --> 00:05:38.905
- We're a modern day Mad Man.

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:40.540
We're a Madison Avenue agency,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.175
the same people that used
to sell commercial time

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.144
on anything you ever did
in radio and television,

00:05:44.144 --> 00:05:48.114
we now do that on Facebook,
Snapchat, Instagram.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:51.084
And we produce the 
creative for the brands

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:53.820
to sell stuff, 
through the phone.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.522
Because Larry, 
and I'd love to get,

00:05:55.522 --> 00:05:56.456
I'd almost want to,

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:57.457
I know we're doing a show here,

00:05:57.457 --> 00:05:58.592
but I'd love to get 
your thoughts,

00:05:58.592 --> 00:06:00.327
maybe after, maybe right now,
who knows.

00:06:00.327 --> 00:06:02.429
I think we're living through
a very interesting moment.

00:06:02.429 --> 00:06:05.865
I believe that the telephone
is becoming the television.

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:08.268
And the television is 
becoming the radio.

00:06:08.268 --> 00:06:10.237
And I've been spending
a lot of time studying

00:06:10.237 --> 00:06:13.506
the transition, in the late
50's, from radio to television.

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:14.708
Because this is the first time

00:06:14.708 --> 00:06:17.310
we've had a platform 
shift in our society

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.079
in a half a century.

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:20.981
And I think it's 
a very big deal.

00:06:20.981 --> 00:06:22.649
And I've been spending an
enormous amount of time,

00:06:22.649 --> 00:06:23.483
the last five years,

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:26.186
trying to be the best
storyteller for that platform.

00:06:26.186 --> 00:06:27.954
- Next, utilizing 
the digital world

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:30.023
for your 
entrepreneurial benefit.

00:06:30.023 --> 00:06:32.459
How social media can
transform your business.

00:06:32.459 --> 00:06:34.236
Stay with us.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:39.866
- We're back with the
incredible Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:06:39.866 --> 00:06:41.301
Is that a Jewish name?

00:06:41.301 --> 00:06:42.235
- You know it's funny,

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:43.169
I know it always 
confuses people.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:44.771
People don't think it, 
but I am.

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:45.605
- [Larry] Okay.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:46.439
(laughter)

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:48.108
The book, 
#AskGaryVee is out now.

00:06:48.108 --> 00:06:50.877
An entrepreneurs 
take on leadership,

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:53.446
social media and self-awareness.

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:55.915
Okay, how do we use social media

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:58.018
to help our business?

00:06:58.018 --> 00:06:59.252
- Well I think we 
first understand

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.620
that social media is 
a slang term

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:03.123
for the current 
state of the internet.

00:07:03.123 --> 00:07:05.492
And when you position
social media that way,

00:07:05.492 --> 00:07:07.127
you take it a lot 
more seriously.

00:07:07.127 --> 00:07:07.961
So step one Larry,

00:07:07.961 --> 00:07:10.130
for 97% of the people 
that are watching,

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:12.065
is to actually take it serious.

00:07:12.065 --> 00:07:13.199
That's number one.

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:15.101
And again, we were talking
as we were getting ready,

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:17.437
a lot of radio people
didn't take television

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:18.872
serious when the 
transition happened.

00:07:18.872 --> 00:07:20.106
That was their loss.

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:20.940
- Correct.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.775
- [Gary] Right?

00:07:21.775 --> 00:07:22.842
That's what's happening 
right now, Larry,

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:23.843
this is historics,

00:07:23.843 --> 00:07:25.879
that history always 
tells you the future.

00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:26.780
And so that's what's happening.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:27.914
So first take it serious.

00:07:27.914 --> 00:07:30.216
Two, understand that Facebook,

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:32.419
and Instagram, and Snapchat,

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:35.522
and YouTube
these are different channels.

00:07:35.522 --> 00:07:37.957
It's the difference 
between CNN and Fox,

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:41.199
and ABC and Sports ESPN.

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:43.697
You've gotta 
understand the context

00:07:43.697 --> 00:07:45.432
of the medium that you're on.

00:07:45.432 --> 00:07:46.766
So when you're story telling

00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:48.702
about your business on YouTube,

00:07:48.702 --> 00:07:50.170
you've gotta produce 
different content

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.372
than when you're putting
a picture on Facebook.

00:07:52.372 --> 00:07:54.908
So again, 
sitting in your presence,

00:07:54.908 --> 00:07:57.510
I almost wanna ask questions
more than do this interview.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.330
I think that people

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.047
underestimate context 
of the medium.

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:03.249
I would assume that when you
interviewed somebody on radio

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:04.851
versus when you 
did it on television,

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:05.851
there's slight differences

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:06.986
'cause they're 
different mediums.

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:07.821
- Slight.

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:09.956
- Slight and it's slight, 
but it's real.

00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:12.325
And in that slightness 
is all the magic.

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:14.561
Number three, it's understanding

00:08:14.561 --> 00:08:16.463
that you have to provide value.

00:08:16.463 --> 00:08:18.264
Too many businesses 
right now on Facebook

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:19.399
and Twitter and Instagram,

00:08:19.399 --> 00:08:21.668
every post they put 
out is buy my stuff,

00:08:21.668 --> 00:08:23.636
buy my stuff, buy my stuff,

00:08:23.636 --> 00:08:25.505
here's where I'm gonna be, 
check me out,

00:08:25.505 --> 00:08:27.474
buy my book, 
check out my experience,

00:08:27.474 --> 00:08:28.742
watch me on my show,

00:08:28.742 --> 00:08:30.510
and nobody's providing values.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:32.278
So the prior book 
I wrote to this was called,

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:34.280
Jab, Jab, Jab, Right Hook.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.416
Give, Give, Give, Ask.

00:08:36.416 --> 00:08:37.583
And it gave people a formula

00:08:37.583 --> 00:08:39.486
of how to put out content

00:08:39.486 --> 00:08:41.254
that actually gave 
people enough value

00:08:41.254 --> 00:08:43.857
that you then had 
them in a consideration

00:08:43.857 --> 00:08:45.091
to buy your stuff.

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:47.360
- But the technology 
changes so much

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.562
that something 
could be new tomorrow,

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:51.765
that wipes out what was
yesterday, right?

00:08:51.765 --> 00:08:54.317
- Tough crap. Right?

00:08:54.317 --> 00:08:56.102
The market is the 
market is the market.

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:58.872
Everyday people put out
shows on radio and television

00:08:58.872 --> 00:09:00.874
that tried to knock 
you off your pedestal.

00:09:00.874 --> 00:09:02.242
You had to become number one,

00:09:02.242 --> 00:09:03.309
stay number one,

00:09:03.309 --> 00:09:04.544
that's the market.

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:07.547
I wish that Twitter wasn't
losing it's leadership role.

00:09:07.547 --> 00:09:09.249
I have 1.2 million 
followers on Twitter.

00:09:09.249 --> 00:09:10.650
I built my brand on Twitter.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.018
I'm dominant on Twitter.

00:09:12.018 --> 00:09:12.986
- I have 2.8.

