- Hustling.

00:18:07.386 --> 00:18:08.254
- Owning the Jets?

00:18:08.254 --> 00:18:09.382
- [Gary] No.
- Not yet.

00:18:09.382 --> 00:18:13.125
- My behavior doesn't map to
owning the Jets in 10 years.

00:18:13.125 --> 00:18:15.828
My behavior maps to 
owning the Jets in 30.

00:18:15.828 --> 00:18:17.196
- Lot of social media questions.

00:18:17.196 --> 00:18:18.965
Serena Brahney on Facebook:

00:18:18.965 --> 00:18:21.734
"When do you know you're
ready to start a business?"

00:18:21.734 --> 00:18:22.568
- If you're asking 
that question,

00:18:22.568 --> 00:18:24.837
you're probably not ready.

00:18:24.837 --> 00:18:26.639
(laughing)

00:18:26.639 --> 00:18:29.875
Larry, I believe a 
purebred entrepreneur

00:18:29.875 --> 00:18:32.211
suffocates in the notion
of doing anything

00:18:32.211 --> 00:18:33.879
but running their own business.

00:18:33.879 --> 00:18:35.247
- @chefchipper on Twitter:

00:18:35.247 --> 00:18:37.984
"What was you favorite
baseball card as a kid?"

00:18:37.984 --> 00:18:41.487
- 1990 Leaf 
Frank Thomas rookie card.

00:18:41.487 --> 00:18:43.889
Me and my friend 
Brandon adored that card.

00:18:43.889 --> 00:18:45.091
- Just saw Frank 
a couple weeks ago.

00:18:45.091 --> 00:18:46.125
- He's a real player.

00:18:46.125 --> 00:18:47.159
He was a real player.

00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:48.427
- Oh, he's in Hall of Fame.

00:18:48.427 --> 00:18:50.963
Bruce MacLelland on the
Larry King Now blog:

00:18:50.963 --> 00:18:52.365
"Do you get political at all?

00:18:52.365 --> 00:18:53.499
"What are your 
thoughts on people

00:18:53.499 --> 00:18:54.700
"from the private sector?

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:55.635
"Donald Trump?"

00:18:55.635 --> 00:18:57.737
- (Sighs) I don't tend 
to get political

00:18:57.737 --> 00:19:00.406
mainly because I've voted
both party lines already

00:19:00.406 --> 00:19:03.209
at this young of 
an age multiple times.

00:19:03.209 --> 00:19:05.211
I'm quite practical 
about politics.

00:19:05.211 --> 00:19:07.446
I wait 'til there's a decision

00:19:07.446 --> 00:19:08.481
and I make the 
decision that I think

00:19:08.481 --> 00:19:11.183
is best in the 
current situation.

00:19:11.183 --> 00:19:12.752
- @BreckLandscape on Twitter:

00:19:12.752 --> 00:19:14.754
"What's more investable:

00:19:14.754 --> 00:19:16.222
"a digital company with millions

00:19:16.222 --> 00:19:18.257
"of users and no profit

00:19:18.257 --> 00:19:19.959
"or one with a few users

00:19:19.959 --> 00:19:22.595
"and millions in profit?"

00:19:22.595 --> 00:19:23.663
- Both

00:19:23.663 --> 00:19:24.530
is true,

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:27.266
but if there's no 
growth at no profit

00:19:27.266 --> 00:19:29.101
with millions of users,

00:19:29.101 --> 00:19:30.369
that's the one 
that you wanna run

00:19:30.369 --> 00:19:31.804
because the upside is so great.

00:19:31.804 --> 00:19:33.139
- Now, Twitter had that, right?

00:19:33.139 --> 00:19:34.607
- Yeah, and look.

00:19:34.607 --> 00:19:36.142
I was an early 
investor in Twitter.

00:19:36.142 --> 00:19:37.743
- Twitter isn't bad, is it?

00:19:37.743 --> 00:19:38.577
- No, it's not bad

00:19:38.577 --> 00:19:39.478
and by the way,

00:19:39.478 --> 00:19:41.514
I made a crap 
load of money, Larry.

00:19:41.514 --> 00:19:42.648
The people who 
invested in Twitter

00:19:42.648 --> 00:19:45.051
before it went 
public made a fortune.

00:19:45.051 --> 00:19:46.852
Twitter's issue is that it needs

00:19:46.852 --> 00:19:50.022
to hold onto the 
attention of its users.

00:19:50.022 --> 00:19:51.557
- Mr. Lou on Facebook asks,

00:19:51.557 --> 00:19:53.526
"In the age of digital media,

00:19:53.526 --> 00:19:55.394
"how do you suggest 
someone maintain

00:19:55.394 --> 00:19:57.396
"a solid print audience?"

00:19:58.564 --> 00:19:59.598
- By making sure, A,

00:19:59.598 --> 00:20:01.834
they are building 
a digital audience,

00:20:01.834 --> 00:20:04.003
and by B, trying to 
find something clever

00:20:04.003 --> 00:20:06.238
that the print 
product can deliver

00:20:06.238 --> 00:20:09.108
that the digital product cannot

00:20:09.108 --> 00:20:10.776
and also this Larry:

00:20:14.013 --> 00:20:15.681
holding your breath.

00:20:15.681 --> 00:20:17.450
The user is dying off

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.051
and when I say dying 
I don't mean literally dying.

00:20:19.051 --> 00:20:21.520
I mean we are watching 
40, 50, 60, 70,

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.073
80-year-olds shift into
digital consumption.

00:20:24.073 --> 00:20:25.558
- But if print is dying,

00:20:25.558 --> 00:20:28.627
why does a Carlos Slim
invest in the New York Times?

00:20:28.627 --> 00:20:29.695
- Because he wants to use

00:20:29.695 --> 00:20:32.031
that to push his propaganda.

00:20:33.766 --> 00:20:34.600
- He's got a vehicle.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.102
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:20:36.102 --> 00:20:37.002
- As Jeff Bezos.

00:20:37.002 --> 00:20:38.003
- Of course.

00:20:39.572 --> 00:20:40.639
Larry, that's the real answer,

00:20:40.639 --> 00:20:41.474
you know it.

00:20:41.474 --> 00:20:43.976
- Yeah, @KJM1016 on Twitter:

00:20:43.976 --> 00:20:45.578
"How would you 
suggest one avoids

00:20:45.578 --> 00:20:47.947
"running their social outlets

00:20:47.947 --> 00:20:50.449
"without making 
it feel like spam?"

00:20:50.449 --> 00:20:51.350
- By not being spam.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.551
Larry, this pisses 
me off all the time.

00:20:52.551 --> 00:20:54.220
People are like, 
"How do I be less sell-y?"

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:55.387
Be less sell-y.

00:20:55.387 --> 00:20:57.723
Provide people actual value.

00:20:58.624 --> 00:20:59.992
If you don't want 
your social media

00:20:59.992 --> 00:21:01.894
to feel spammy, 
why don't you talk

00:21:01.894 --> 00:21:03.229
about putting out content

00:21:03.229 --> 00:21:04.964
and acting like a media company

00:21:04.964 --> 00:21:07.399
instead of acting 
like a salesperson?

00:21:07.399 --> 00:21:09.435
- Jon Crabtree on 
the Larry King Now blog:

00:21:09.435 --> 00:21:11.237
"Do you see any 
new apps emerging

00:21:11.237 --> 00:21:15.274
"as game changers 
in the next few years?"

00:21:15.274 --> 00:21:16.075
- Like I mentioned,

00:21:16.075 --> 00:21:17.476
I'm very hot on music.ly.

00:21:17.476 --> 00:21:18.511
I also think Anchor,

00:21:18.511 --> 00:21:20.012
this audio app is 
quite interesting.

00:21:20.012 --> 00:21:20.846
- What is that?

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:24.049
- Think of it as voice Twitter,

00:21:24.049 --> 00:21:25.518
so you instead of tweeting,

00:21:25.518 --> 00:21:26.986
you're doing it by voice,

00:21:26.986 --> 00:21:28.187
so you literally put up
the phone to yourself

00:21:28.187 --> 00:21:29.421
and say, "Today I'm thinking,"

00:21:29.421 --> 00:21:30.523
and quite interesting.

00:21:30.523 --> 00:21:31.524
It's very, very early,

00:21:31.524 --> 00:21:34.360
but I'm keeping a 
very keen eye on it.

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.862
- Who invents these things?

00:21:36.862 --> 00:21:40.166
- Youngsters a lot of times
who don't know any better.

00:21:40.166 --> 00:21:42.234
You know what I would 
tell you and I think

00:21:42.234 --> 00:21:43.269
this will make sense to you.

00:21:43.269 --> 00:21:45.938
I look at it very 
similar to music genres.

00:21:45.938 --> 00:21:47.606
I think of it as like The Clash

00:21:47.606 --> 00:21:50.142
and Kurt Cobain, and Run-DMC.

00:21:50.142 --> 00:21:51.944
I think it's people who
come up, they don't see what

00:21:51.944 --> 00:21:54.813
they want, and they create it.

00:21:54.813 --> 00:21:57.950
- @SoulmanScofield, Do you
think you'll ever retire

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:00.553
or do you enjoy 
working too much?

00:22:00.553 --> 00:22:02.955
- I think when 
I'm sitting here with you,

00:22:02.955 --> 00:22:05.191
and I know all the
context I know about you,

00:22:05.191 --> 00:22:06.892
and the fact that you
pretty much have interviewed

00:22:06.892 --> 00:22:09.762
anybody who's important in
the last 50 god damn years,

00:22:09.762 --> 00:22:11.130
the thing that most excites me,

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.031
is that you're 
sitting right here,

00:22:12.031 --> 00:22:13.532
right now and interviewing me.

00:22:13.532 --> 00:22:15.701
I have no interest in
doing anything other.

00:22:15.701 --> 00:22:17.469
That to me is the most
interesting thing

00:22:17.469 --> 00:22:18.971
that's happening 
in this room right now,

00:22:18.971 --> 00:22:20.406
is that while 
I'm doing this interview,

00:22:20.406 --> 00:22:22.474
the parallel part 
of my brain is saying,

00:22:22.474 --> 00:22:24.162
"Damn, this is the guy.

00:22:24.162 --> 00:22:26.011
"This is exactly what 
I'm gonna be doing."

00:22:26.011 --> 00:22:26.846
- Are you married?

00:22:26.846 --> 00:22:27.680
- I am.

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:28.317
- [Larry] You have children?

00:22:28.317 --> 00:22:29.782
- I do.

00:22:29.782 --> 00:22:31.016
- How old are the children?

00:22:31.016 --> 00:22:32.218
- Six and three.

00:22:32.218 --> 00:22:33.052
- Boy and a girl?

00:22:33.052 --> 00:22:34.720
- Mhmmm, girl, boy.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.324
- Alright, how do your thoughts
on your ability in business

00:22:38.324 --> 00:22:40.593
to raising a family?

00:22:40.593 --> 00:22:42.094
Do you use the 
principles at home--

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:43.395
- I do.
- [Larry] Yeah?

00:22:43.395 --> 00:22:46.665
- I think first of all,
I'm an EQ-HR-driven CEO.

00:22:46.665 --> 00:22:48.334
I think I win not on my IQ,

00:22:48.334 --> 00:22:49.768
but on my 
emotional intelligence.

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:53.005
I think that I'm very in
tune to people's feelings.

00:22:53.005 --> 00:22:55.207
I try to reverse engineer
what they're about.

00:22:55.207 --> 00:22:57.509
For example, I have 
no interest in my kids

00:22:57.509 --> 00:22:59.178
being anything but who they are.

00:22:59.178 --> 00:23:02.982
My mom gifted me by
parenting me to my strengths.

00:23:02.982 --> 00:23:05.985
If my kids are artists or
want to be in the non-profit

00:23:05.985 --> 00:23:08.954
sector or climb mountains, 
I'm all-in.

00:23:08.954 --> 00:23:10.623
I want them to do them.

00:23:10.623 --> 00:23:14.560
The one line in the sand I have
is no eighth place trophies.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:16.462
Lizzie knows, and now I'm
looking at the camera,

00:23:16.462 --> 00:23:18.030
'cause I want her 
to see my eyes again,

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:20.266
no eighth place trophies.

00:23:20.266 --> 00:23:23.435
Life is about winning and
losing. It's binary, right?

00:23:23.435 --> 00:23:24.904
So as long as my kids know that,

00:23:24.904 --> 00:23:25.971
I don't care what they do.

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:26.972
They don't need to make money.

00:23:26.972 --> 00:23:29.775
They don't need to be
entrepreneurs, but to win,

00:23:29.775 --> 00:23:30.909
there's a real market.

00:23:30.909 --> 00:23:33.712
To be a great artist, to be
great at helping other people,

00:23:33.712 --> 00:23:34.880
you have to be great at it.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:37.049
- Does your wife get
involved in your business?

00:23:37.049 --> 00:23:40.119
- No, we have a very
strong line in the sand.

00:23:40.119 --> 00:23:41.887
My parents had that.

00:23:41.887 --> 00:23:43.889
Her parents had 
that for the most part,

00:23:43.889 --> 00:23:45.291
so I think we're 
just kind of acting

00:23:45.291 --> 00:23:47.626
on what we saw behaviorally.

00:23:49.428 --> 00:23:51.997
Look, when you're 
the CEO of a company,

00:23:51.997 --> 00:23:53.899
when you're the top dog,

00:23:53.899 --> 00:23:56.702
you're only in the
negative issues business.

00:23:56.702 --> 00:23:58.304
I'm a firefighter.

00:23:58.304 --> 00:24:00.039
All I'm dealing 
with is headaches.

00:24:00.039 --> 00:24:01.173
What's gonna happen right now?

00:24:01.173 --> 00:24:03.175
I'm gonna step outta here, and
I'm gonna look at my phone,

00:24:03.175 --> 00:24:04.710
and there's gonna be seven
things I have to deal with

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:06.812
that isn't a fun 
thing to deal with.

00:24:06.812 --> 00:24:10.015
So to come home and pour that
on my wife isn't interesting.

00:24:10.015 --> 00:24:11.917
I don't have 
anything positive to say.

00:24:11.917 --> 00:24:13.385
On a micro level, 
it's all negative.

00:24:13.385 --> 00:24:16.655
It's the macro 
level that's awesome.

00:24:16.655 --> 00:24:17.656
- Gary, you're incredible.

00:24:17.656 --> 00:24:18.490
Great meeting you.
- Thank you, brother,

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:19.391
nice meeting you.

00:24:19.391 --> 00:24:20.426
- [Larry] This is the
first of many meetings.

00:24:20.426 --> 00:24:21.660
- Thank you, my friend.

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:24.979
(upbeat music)

